in the multiverse bending spider-man no  way home one spider-man variant gets his  long-awaited redemption but does the  other one lose the love of his life  i know you predicted i disappoint you  bingo  fans blood went cold the minute toby  maguire's peter said the two most  dreaded words someone can say regarding  his romance with mj it's complicated  spider-man fans everywhere first started  rooting for these kooky kids back in  2002 and spent three films watching  there will they or won't they romance  play out on screen after all of that  teenage angst followed by an implied  happy ending this one liner comes across  as a slap in the face look we get it  adult life is difficult enough when  you're not a web-slinging superhero plus  the third film showed how tricky it is  dating someone with a double identity  however no way home doesn't really have  the right to retcon one of the most  iconic couples in superhero history  i can't survive without you  additionally the throwaway line doesn't  add anything to the story and only  confuses audiences we also hear peter  talk about mj saying we made it work not  only is the past tense of that sentiment  confusing it makes it unclear whether  peter and mj are still going the  distance basically the moment cast a  dark cloud over an otherwise epic cameo  happy and aunt may aren't exactly a  couple written in the stars and happy is  not a character who's always taken  seriously but he still deserves to be  treated as more than a vehicle for  comedy relief it's pretty clear that  happy is a tad more invested in his  non-relationship with may than she is  but no way home takes it to a whole  other level peter comes home after his  identity as spider-man is outed to have  another awkward family sit down happy  amazed breakup tries to ease attention  of peter's situation but in hindsight  it's not very funny happy certainly  needs to accept that they're not in the  same page romantically  mae could break things off with happy a  bit more tactfully may's death is one of  the most devastating scenes in tom  holland's trilogy but her death also  means that her savage breakup with happy  is the last memory he has of her he  never really gets the closure he  deserves and sure that's life but it's  still a pretty sad place to leave happy  at the end of this movie  when it comes to the villains and no way  home the majority are given ample space  to explore their psyches and work  through some of their trauma however the  attention is largely skewed towards sam  raimi's set of villains whereas green  goblin and dark arc truly work to become  better people we miss out on those  nuances with the lizard and electro from  the amazing spider-man series granted  both the amazing spider-man villains are  developed far more in their original  films than raymie's set but fans would  still like to see more from them in no  way home the lizard and electro are  opposed to tom holland's pizza helping  them but they have no choice in the end  all three peters force them into a cure  which isn't a great message when helping  people is the tagline then with little  build up everyone is happy to be back to  normal the movie does what it can with  its time but it would be nice to see a  bit more depth from the amazing  spider-man crew and even raymie sandman  we get one great moment with elektro  when he remarks his surprise that  spider-man isn't black but hopes that  there is a universe where he is of  course spidey fans know that this is a  shout out to miles morales who recently  appeared in spider-man into the  spider-verse  okay we get it tony sark was responsible  for some pretty incredible technological  advances during his time at stark  industries of course that unbridled  power would attract villains however  some things are sacred like tony's arc  reactor that's because it's a  miniaturized dark reactor  it makes sense that mcu creators want to  keep iron man's legacy alive in new  projects but there are other ways to  accomplish that without insulting his  memory when electro uses tony's arc  reactor it's hard not to take the  dastardly use of iron man's most iconic  piece of tech personally his arc reactor  is what kept the mcu's first superhero  alive and post-mortem it would be nice  if he could retire that particular piece  of superhero history rather than  corrupting and distorting its purpose so  long after tony's death iron man's  memory deserves more than being a tool  for villains to take advantage of let  the man rest in peace  doctor strange may be a pro at chaotic  spell work with unintended consequences  burt has botched jobs and no way home  takes the cake sure we need a catalyst  for the multiverse villains to appear in  this world but even for strange this is  a lot when peter continually tries to  intervene drawing stranger's spell it  becomes horribly unstable and blows up  in both of their faces but at the end of  the day strange is an adult with more  than enough experience to know better  than to let peter meddle with his spells  he caters to peter's whims multiple  times until peter himself changes the  incantation but it should never get that  far strange blames peter for the bungled  spell work but look who his role model  is  we started getting some visitors  even worse more than a few plot holes  emerge regarding strange's clumsy magic  wielding when all is said and done yet  the worst of it comes at the end of the  film when peter asked strange to do a  spell to make everyone forget that he's  spider-man even michelle and ned however  it doesn't just make them forget  spidey's identity they don't remember  peter at all even students he's gone to  school with for years who knew peter  before his spidey identity forgot him  all in all it's frustrating to end the  trilogy with more questions than answers  with a completely trusting partnership  tom holland's peter has cultivated in  his own mj it's disheartening when he  takes away her autonomy over their  relationship michelle knows the risks of  being spider-man's girlfriend and she's  more than capable of holding her own but  once everyone's memory of peter goes  away with stranger spell he decides that  she should live her life without him  peter made a promise to michelle before  the spell that he would find her and  tell her everything that happened so  they could work on rebuilding their  relationship instead he pockets his  speech and walks away taking her choice  away from her which is undeniably  heartbreaking and cruel  taking three betas from spider-man  universes hailing from different decades  could have ended in disaster still  writers eric summers and chris mckenna  seamlessly pull it off alongside  director john watts and of course the  web-slinging actors themselves without  missing a beat toby maguire and andrew  garfield dusted off their cobwebs and  delivered heartfelt performances with  their usual dash of humor and one-liners  fans can even argue that their  respective cameos are even better than  their original roles these cameos could  have been shallow and fleeting but  instead they each have meaningful  character arcs in their quest to help  the next generation spider-man and that  credit goes to everyone who worked on  the project it would have been such a  letdown to see the ogs come back for a  frivolous minute or two but we get to  know this older iteration of our heroes  and it's a gift  toby maguire's peter parker spends a  good part of the franchise struggling  with his thirst for vengeance between  his quest to find and kill uncle ben's  killer to being somewhat responsible for  norman osborn's death quite a few people  have died at the hands of our friendly  neighbourhood spider-man turns out it  was really some man who shot uncle ben  but whatever spider revenge  in no way home we see some holland  spider-man grapple with a similar  mission to even the score after green  goblin kills aunt may however maguire's  beater can't stand the idea of watching  another peter go down this dark path and  stops him from becoming a cold-blooded  killer the moment our 2002 peter parker  convinces peter to spare norman he hands  a baton to holland allowing a new  generation to take it from here watching  maguire's peter get some closure and  peace through helping a younger version  of himself is a beautiful moment that  mends some of his old scars one of the  biggest flaws superhero films fall into  even in modern times is making its women  helpless damsels who need saving every 5  minutes however in no way home michelle  and peter save each other on countless  occasions in an equal partnership the  update on the usual trope is a  refreshing signal that times are  changing and so must our superheroes  everyone needs help sometimes and it's  nice to see peter accept assistance from  someone he loves instead of pushing it  away if you're going to have a woman by  your side when you're fighting crimes  and taking names michelle jones is the  best out there and she's not afraid to  call peter out when he needs it either  what are you hiding  peter  when you grow up with the avengers it's  easy to get into a mentality that every  problem needs solving via magic or a  heavy duty piece of stark industries  tech however the mcu's peter parker is  still a teenager with typical human  issues like not getting into college  sure the reason he doesn't get into mit  is rooted in the fact that he spent the  last years of his life running around a  spider-man still before trying to  magically whammy the world with an  amnesia spell peter really should have  contacted the school to explain his  position once dr strange calls him out  on a serious lack of critical thinking  peter does find someone at mit he  complete his case to using his typical  charm he wins her over  dark arc is one of spider-man's most  fleshed out foes when it comes to the  og's spider-man trilogy his journey has  three parts dog's initial introduction  his villainous descent and ultimately  reclaiming his kind-hearted nature and  defeating himself no way home expands on  his already developed dark and makes it  even better by bringing fans more of ox  humanity no way home allows him to truly  become the hero and save his fellow  villains and the process many of  spider-man's villains deserve much more  space to explore their humanity and  villainhood than the original films  allowed this is most definitely true of  the first film's green goblin even  before norman turns into the goblin he  isn't winning any father of the year  awards the villain barely grapples with  his humanity even when he's fighting off  his evil alter ego and it's hard to root  for the guy when he has so little  substance no way home corrects that by  giving norman proper space to make  better choices and access his humanity  both villains get concrete storylines  that correct some of their  superficiality in the og's  gwen stacy's death in the amazing  spider-man 2 is one of the most gripping  and heartbreaking scenes in any of the  spidey films released before no way home  many fans don't give garfield the credit  to spider-man deserves but he nails this  moment and its 2.0 counterpart and no  way home when michelle falls off the  statue of liberty in an identical way to  gwen it's not her own pizza who saves  her but garfield's setting aside his own  trauma he leaps into action and catches  michelle to spare this universe as peter  from going through the same eternal  grief and guilt he did no way home gives  garfield's peter the nuanced and deep  ending he's always deserved it's clear  that peter hasn't moved on from what  happened to gwen and saving michelle  gives him the closure he needs to move  on with his life and start living it  fully again fans of andrew garfield's  take on spider-man couldn't be more  grateful check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about spider-man no way home are  coming soon subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]

